ass=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6887em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mbin mtight\">∗</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≡</span></span></span></span> language <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(b^*ab^*a(a+b)^*</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6887em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mbin mtight\">∗</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6887em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mbin mtight\">∗</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mclose\"><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6887em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mbin mtight\">∗</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> in that these are both languages with at least 2 'a's.</p>\n<p><strong>SKELETON-></strong> <em>(arbitrary)(whatever is needed)(arbitrary)</em> this is just one way of thinking about a regular expression</p>\n<hr>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>UP NEXT:</strong> <a href=\"/notes/chapter-12\">Determining whether or not two regular expressions define the same language or not</a></p>",
  "rawMarkdown": "\nRegular expressions are symbols that allow us to define a language without using complicated and sometimes verbose symbols and notations.\n\nThe main notations we will use to generate regular expressions will be the [two closures we have already studied](/notes/kleenes-and-positive-closure). We know the Kleene's closure denotes an infinite set starting with a null and a Positive Closure simply doesn't have the null string. \n\n**READ:** [Defining a language associated with any regular expression (too lazy to write down)](/notes/introduction-to-computer-theorypdf)\n\nThe best way to understand regular expressions is just by doing examples. We already know what notation we will use. There is now a new concept that we need to understand. \n\n**e.g1.** Let $L = \\{aa, aba, abba ,abbba, abbbba ...\\}$\n- For this example, the regular expression would be $ab^*a$  which means a string starting with a, having an infinite amount of bs and then ending with an a as well.\n\n**e.g2.**  $a^*b^* \\ne (ab)^*$  \n- The first RE creates the set $L = \\{\\wedge, a, b, aa, bb, ab, aaa, aab, abb, bbb...\\}$\n - The second RE creates the set $L = \\{\\wedge, ab, abab, ababab, abababab...\\}$\n\n**e.g3.** Let $T = \\{a,c,ab,cb,abb,cbb,abbb,cbbb...\\}$\n- In a set like this, we can deduce that each word needs to start with either a or c, followed by an infinite number of bs. The RE for this kind of a set would be $(a+c)b^*$ where the operands behind and front of the + symbol means \"either a or c\"\n\n**e.g4.** Let $T = \\{aaa,aab,aba,abb,baa,bab,bba,bbb\\}$\n- Again, a simple deduction would lead us to conclude that this finite set can either start with a or b, have either a or b in the middle, and end with either a or b\n- Therefore, the RE would be $(a+b)(a+b)(a+b) \\equiv (a+b)^3$\n\n**e.g5.** These two examples are really neat. Should be read from the book [here](/notes/introduction-to-computer-theorypdf) onwards for a complete and better understanding.\n\nWe can also find **equivalent expressions** which define the same language in different ways. \n\n**e.g6.** language $((a+b)^*a(a+b)^*a(a+b)^* \\equiv$ language $(b^*ab^*a(a+b)^*$ in that these are both languages with at least 2 'a's.\n\n**SKELETON->** *(arbitrary)(whatever is needed)(arbitrary)* this is just one way of thinking about a regular expression\n\n\n- - -\n- - - -\n**UP NEXT:** [Determining whether or not two regular expressions define the same language or not](/notes/chapter-12)\n\n",
